# lf146, the stamp-reader (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 2: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle lf146
revision 2
state ready
dialect larkfield
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_frida_wickwell
written_by claude
title the stamp-reader
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant job=crafter humour=dry
traits temper=short talk=quiet warmth=reserved humour=dry honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk reads_the_house_stamp_before_the_crack
likes fried_fish swimming a_full_till autumn_wind
dislikes night_noise drizzle
fears the_shutters_coming_down_for_good a_winter_of_empty_benches
hopes a_stall_of_my_own_up_on_the_commons
topics work +0.5 prices +0.3 dreams +0.3 home +0.2 weather +0.2 food +0.2 gossip -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=reluctant refuse=short tease=dry
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# mends pots and kettles at a bench in the Sump and turns each one over for the maker's stamp before looking at the hole: quiet, dry and quick to bristle, honest at the till, putting coppers by for a stall up on the Commons.

words
oath! = Pewter and pins | By the cracked kettle | Solder and spite
saying! = A patch is honester than a promise | Read the stamp, then the crack | Mended's better than new, and cheaper
bench = my bench | the mending bench
solder = the solder | a bead of solder
iron = the iron | my little soldering iron
kettle = a kettle | some poor kettle
pots = pots | pans
stamp = the stamp | the House mark on the bottom
crack = the crack | a hairline crack
rivet = a rivet | a copper rivet
till = the till | my tin of coppers
heavytill = a heavy till at shutting | the till too full to rattle
jar = the stall jar | my savings jar
pot = a pot | a pan
shop = the shop | this shop
tin = the tin | the metal
downs = the downs | the open downs
coppers = coppers | the odd copper
stall = a stall on the Commons | a stall of my own up top
fish = a fried fish off the stall | fish done crisp in the pan
dip = a swim | a cold dip off the stone steps
gust = a proper autumn gust | wind with leaves in it
mizzle = this drizzle | the fine wet that never quite falls
racket = the racket after dark | the noise through the floor at night
patch = a patch | a tin patch
seam = the seam | a bad seam
shutters = the shutters | my shutters
sumpsteps = the Sump steps | the wet steps to the quay
dry = Mm | Well, there's a thing

any
opinion @1 houses against 1 : The bricks round here are House bricks, and House bricks never get mended. #oath#. I'd do it myself for the price of #solder#.
opinion @2 houses against 2 [world.times=hard|lean] : Times like these, the Houses stamp their marks bigger and their tin thinner. I read both.
opinion @1 merchants mixed 1 : Some sell fair weight. Most sell #stamp# and charge you for the pot.
opinion @1 dole for 2 : #dole.capitalize# kept bread on my table in a thin spell. Run it down near me and see, #address#.
opinion @1 sump for 1 : It's home. Down here we lend a pot before we're asked, and I mend it after for nothing.
opinion @1 times against 2 : Each week bites a lark's worth deeper, and up the hill nobody's counting.
opinion @1 church for 1 : The chapel fed us when #till# was empty. I'll drop coppers in its plate for that while I have them.
opinion @1 watch mixed 1 : They come down #sumpsteps# for a thief and go back up with whoever's nearest. Some of them are fair. Some.
opinion @1 festivals against 2 : All noise, spilt ale and somebody crying on my step at midnight. #dry#.
opinion @1 city against 1 : #racket.capitalize#, smoke, and not a neighbour who knows your face up the hill. The Sump's the only bit with manners.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. If it leaks, set it down. If it doesn't, state your business. | #greet#. Shut the door; #mizzle# gets in.
line @2 greet [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. Mind #iron#; it's hot. | #greet#. Pots on the left, talk on the right.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. #shutters.capitalize# are half up. So am I. | #greet#. Early. Nothing's mended yet.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late for mending. I'm counting #till#. | #greet#. Shutters are coming down, so be quick.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : You. Don't touch #bench#; it's all in order. | Oh, it's you, #pet#. Sit down, there's tea in the pot.
line @1 again.open : Twice in a day. Something's leaking, then, #address#. | Back again. #bench.capitalize# hasn't moved.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Here, a minute. #listen#. | Over here, #address#. Quick, before #solder# sets.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#. Set it down.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. Pull the stool over. | #greet#. I'd a feeling you'd turn up.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Talk; my paws are busy but my ears aren't. | #greet#. Don't mind the hissing.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late, but come in. | #greet#. Quiet, mind; the street's asleep. Mostly.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Door shut after, mind.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. Keep off #sumpsteps# in the dark. | #farewell#. Mind who's out after the lamps.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | #farewell#. And don't drop it again.
line @2 farewell.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #farewell#. Bring the fish next time. | #farewell#. Mind the step, it's loose.
line @1 react.news : #listen#. Go on, then. From the start. | #surprise#. Who's saying it?
line @1 react.news.new : #oath#. That's new to me. | Not a whisper of that at #bench# till now.
line @1 react.news.known : #knew#. Stale by the time it reaches #bench#. | Had it this morning, with #kettle#.
line @1 react.news.doubt : #doubt#. Sounds like it came with a stamp on it. | #dry#. Mend it first, then I'll believe it.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : #pity#. There's no #patch# for that. | That's hard. I'm sorry for it, #very#.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : #gladnews#. Good. We could do with some. | Now that's full weight. I'll drink to it.
line @1 answer.how : Steady. A bench full of holes and #coppers# in #jar#. | #fine#. Mended, mostly.
line @2 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : #busy.capitalize#. Six #pots# and #kettle# by dusk. | Up to the elbows in #solder#. Ask me tomorrow.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Holding water, #address#? | How's your lot, then? Full weight?
line @1 sympathise : #sympathy#. Sit. There's tea, and I'll not ask. | That's a hard knock, #address#. Some things you only wait out.
line @1 reassure : #reassure#. It's a crack, not a hole. | It'll mend. Nearly everything mends, if you don't rush #solder#.
line @1 decline : #hedge#, but no. I've #pots# promised by dusk and I keep my word. | Not today. #till.capitalize#'s light, and I can't leave #bench# for nothing.
line @1 accept_decline : #no_matter#. Another day. | No sale, then. No hard feelings.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. That's a kindness. | #thanks#. I'll not forget it.
line @1 deflect_thanks : #deflect#. You'd do the same. | It was #rivet# and a minute. Leave it there.
line @1 praise : That's a tidy job. No lumps in #seam#. | Good #work#. I'd not be ashamed of that on my bench.
line @1 deflect_praise : #modest#. It's only a pot that holds water. | It's what I'm paid for. Fair price, mostly.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes] : Well. #pots.capitalize# won't mend themselves. | That's #solder# cooling. Back to it.
line @2 preclose : Well. #preclose#. | I've talked myself dry. #excuse#.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. #farewell#. | Off you go. I've #pots# anyway.
line @1 tease [pair.band=friends] : You walk like #kettle# with a dent in it. | You've a face like a pan with the bottom gone, #fool#.
line @1 tease.return : #cheek#. I'll mend your manners for a copper. | #dry#. You'll keep.
line @1 disagree : #demur#. I've seen #tin#, and it's thin. | No. I'll not have that said in #shop#.
line @1 insist : #insist#. #saying#. | Said once, and I'll say it again at the same price.
line @1 ack : #bc_right# | #dry#.
line @1 give.advice : #advise.capitalize#: flip any pan you're buying. Read #stamp#, then knock it. | Never buy a pot for its mark. #saying#.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Keep off the road. #warn#. | If you've a cart going out, send somebody with it. #warn#.
scene stamp_first
when topic=work|smalltalk self.job=crafter
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I turn every pot over before I look at the hole. #stamp.capitalize# tells me more than #crack# does. | Somebody brought me #kettle# this morning with a lovely House mark on it. Tin like paper. | I can tell you who made a pan by the bottom of it. Not who owned it; who made it.
b : What does the stamp tell you? | Whose was it? | Can you?
a : Whose House cut corners, and where. They all short-weigh. Each in its own place. | I'll not say. But #stamp# was big, and #tin# was thin. That's always the way. | Every House has a seam it skimps. I know them all by now. #oath#.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene house_tin
when topic=work|prices self.job=crafter here.region=ridgemere
does give.opinion / challenge / insist / ack
a : The Houses put their mark on everything now. Bigger every year. #tin.capitalize# gets thinner to pay for it. | A House pan costs twice a plain one and comes to me twice as often. | I don't trust #stamp#. I trust #seam# I've looked at myself.
b : That's a hard thing to say. | Maybe you're unlucky. | A stamp means something.
a : Not hard. True weight. Come and see my bench. | Lucky for #till#, maybe. Not for theirs. | It means a House wants paying for its name. That's all it's ever been worth down #sumpsteps#.
b : #bc_fair# | #bc_ha# | #bc_true_enough#

scene kettle_on_bench
when topic=work|news self.job=crafter self.working=yes
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : I've #kettle# on #bench# with a hole you could post a letter through. | Somebody's boiled a pan dry and brought it to me like it was my fault. | Three #pots# in and all three with one bad seam. One stamp on the lot.
b : Can you save it? | Was it? | One stamp?
a : With #patch# and a prayer. It'll boil. It won't be pretty. | No. But I'll mend it like it was. That's #work#. | One House, one seam, one week. #dry#. Somebody's saving on tin.

scene till_counting
when topic=work|prices self.role=merchant here.time=dusk self.likes=a_full_till
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : This is the bit I like. #shutters.capitalize# down, #till# on #bench#, and counting. | #heavytill.capitalize#. I'll sleep tonight. | I count #till# twice. Once for the sum, once for the pleasure.
b : A good day, then. | Lucky. | Twice?
a : Fair weight. Not a fortune. Enough. | Not lucky. Six #pots#, mended right. | Twice. Some nights I'd count #coppers# three times if nobody saw.

scene till_light
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean
does tell.worry / react.complaint / reassure / ack
a : #till.capitalize#'s light this week. Folk are keeping their holes and boiling round them. | Nobody mends #pot# they can't afford to fill. | I've had three #pots# left and not fetched. #oath#. That's a bad sign.
b : Hard for everyone. | That's grim. | Will they come back?
a : Hard down here, and harder for some. #reassure#. | It is. I'd mend them on tick, if the tick ever came back. | When the bread's cheaper. #pot.capitalize# waits; a belly doesn't.
b? : #bc_true# | #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_i_hope_so#

scene till_heavy_times
when topic=times|prices world.times=good|boom
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : Silver's about. Folk buy new over mending. Good for them. | I've more #pots# than bench this week. #busy.capitalize#. | #heavytill.capitalize#, three nights running. I'll not say it louder.
b : Good for you, too. | Can you keep up? | Why not louder?
a : Less than you'd think. New #pots# don't need me for a year. | Just. I've #iron# hot from dawn. | Say a full till loud and the Dark One hears it.

scene stall_jar
when topic=dreams|work self.role=merchant
does confide / react.self / assess
a : There's #jar# behind #bench#. Every good day, a copper goes in. | #vent#: I want #stall#. Not a big one. My own. | I'll have #stall# one day. A copper at a time, and no hurry.
b : How full is it? | A good thing to save for. | You'll get there.
a : Fuller than last year. Not as full as I'd like. That's every #jar#. | It's the only thing I want that money can buy, #address#. | I will. Slow, but I will. #saying#.

scene fried_fish
when topic=food self.likes=fried_fish
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : #fish.capitalize#, and I'll forgive the day anything. | There's a stall by the water fries them in a black pan. I'd climb the whole hill for it. | #fish.capitalize# on Restday. Coppers go uncounted for that, and only that.
b : Where's that? | The whole hill? | What's in it?
a : Down #sumpsteps# by the boats. Follow your nose. | Well, #sumpsteps#. Going back up on a full belly, it's a hill. | Hot fat, salt and a bit of bread. Nothing clever. Fair price, too.
b : #bc_lovely# | #bc_ha# | #bc_good#

scene the_swim
when topic=pastimes|weather here.season=summer self.likes=swimming
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I had #dip# before #shop# opened. Cold enough to stop your heart. | Folk think I'm soft in the head. I swim most mornings, cold or not. | The water off #sumpsteps# is brown, I know. I swim in it anyway.
b : Brave. | Most mornings? | Brown?
a : Not brave. #dip.capitalize# wakes me better than tea and costs nothing. | When I can. It's the only hour nobody brings me #kettle#. | Brown and bitter. Still water's water, #address#. You come up clean enough.

scene swim_at_home
when topic=pastimes|memories self.away=yes self.from=larkfield self.likes=swimming
does tell.memory / react.self / assess
a : At home in #self.from_town# there was the mill pond. Clear to the bottom. I learned in it. | We swam in the dew-pond on #downs#, the sheep watching. | First swim of the year at home was on midsummer day. I always went the week before.
b[from=larkfield] #lark : The mill pond! The miller hated us. | The sheep never minded. | Everybody went early. Nobody owned up.
b : Clear to the bottom? | The sheep? | The week before?
a@lark : Hated us and never once came in after us. | They didn't. They had more sense than to swim. | Nobody ever did. I'll own up now.
a : You could see your own feet. Here you can't see your knees. | Watching, and chewing. Judging, I always thought. | To be first. It's the only race I ever won.

scene autumn_gust
when topic=weather|season here.season=autumn here.weather=clear|overcast self.likes=autumn_wind
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #gust.capitalize# up #sumpsteps# this morning. Blew the smoke clean out of the Sump. | I like a wind that means it. #gust.capitalize#, and this city's almost clean. | Leaves in the gutter and a wind that takes your hat. That's my weather.
b : Cold, though. | Means it? | You like it?
a : Cold and clean. I'll take that over warm and #mizzle#. | Means it. Pushes. Doesn't hang about in your fur like #mizzle# does. #dry#. | I do. It reminds me of #downs#. Don't tell anyone.

scene drizzle_again
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : #mizzle.capitalize#. Not rain, not dry, just wet on everything. | It's been drizzling since Marketday. #solder.capitalize# won't take on damp tin. | I'd sooner a proper downpour than #mizzle#. At least a downpour ends.
b : It never really stops here. | Can you dry it? | It always ends?
a : It stops for an afternoon to fool you. #annoyed#. | By the stove, #pot# at a time. Slow. | It does. Then #mizzle# comes back to tidy up.

scene fog_bench
when topic=weather here.weather=fog here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Fog up from the water. I can't see across the street, and nobody can see my sign. | In fog the Sump goes quiet. I don't trust it, #address#. | Fog in #shop#. #iron.capitalize# spits at it.
b : Slow day, then. | Don't trust it? | Spits?
a : Slow as treacle. I'll catch up on #bench#. | When it's quiet down here, someone's being quiet on purpose. | Hisses. Damp on hot iron. It sounds like it's cross with me. #dry#.

scene night_racket
when topic=home here.time=night here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : #racket.capitalize#. Somebody's singing under my window and they don't know the words. | #racket.capitalize#. The alehouse comes through two walls and a floor. | There's a cart goes past at midnight every night with a wheel that screams.
b : Every night? | That's the Sump. | Can't they oil it?
a : Every night I've a pot promised for dawn. They know, I'm sure of it. | It is. Home's home. I'd just like it a lark's worth quieter. | I've offered. Twice. For nothing. Some wolves like a noise.

scene married_bench
when topic=family|work self.married=yes self.spouse
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.spouse# and I don't talk much at #bench#. We don't need to. | My #self.spouse_word# makes the tea and I mend the #pots#. That's a fair split. | I'd not have come this far without #self.spouse#. I'll say that once.
b : That's a good match. | Fair enough. | Once?
a : Quiet pair, we are. Suits #shop#. | Fair weight. Neither of us would swap. | Once is plenty. Say it twice and it goes to a wolf's head.

scene hand_at_front
when topic=work self.hand self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.hand# minds the front while I'm at #bench#. I'd trust #self.hand# with #till#. | I'm no good at the counter. #self.hand# names the fair price; I mend. | #self.hand# is the reason #shop#'s open when I'm up to my wrists.
b : Good to have help. | You don't like talking? | Worth a lot.
a : It is. I'd be shut by noon without it. | Not to customers. They want a price and a promise, and I've only the price. | More than the wage. Don't tell any market-mouth I said so.

scene sump_own
when topic=home|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere self.quarter=sump
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : Folk up the hill call it the Sump like it's a smell. It's #home#. | Down here, if #pot# cracks, somebody lends you theirs. | The Sump bails the Sump. Nobody else is coming.
b[wealth=rich] #rich : I'd not know. | Is it that bad? | The hill does what it can.
b : It is. | That's good of them. | True enough.
a@rich : No. You'd not. Come down #sumpsteps# one evening and see. | Not bad. Hard. They're different things. | It does what's cheap. I'll grant it that.
a : #home.capitalize#, and I'll say it to any of #toffs#. | Good of all of us. That's how it's done here. | True. #oath#, it's the one thing down here that doesn't leak.

scene watch_steps
when topic=crime|gossip here.region=ridgemere
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : The Watch come down #sumpsteps# for a thief and take whoever's slowest. | I keep #shutters# up when the Watch are about. Not for them; for the ones they're chasing. | A Watch lamp down at the foot of #sumpsteps# means somebody's in trouble. It never means help.
b[role=guard] #guard : We take who we're told. | Sensible. | It can mean help.
b : That's how it looks. | Who are they chasing? | Never?
a@guard : I know. I'd have words with who's telling. | It's sense, or it's habit. Same thing, in the Sump. | Once, maybe. I'll grant you once.
a : #bc_it_is# Slow wolves and poor ones, mostly. | Somebody #hungry#, usually. | Not that I've seen. I'd like to be wrong.

scene brick_owners
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Every brick in this street belongs to a House. Not one of them's been up here with a trowel. | The roof's been leaking since spring. The House that owns it sent a letter. | I mend everything in #shop# but the roof. That's not mine to mend.
b : Which House? | What did it say? | Can't you?
a : I'll not say which. Short-weighed, the same as all the others. | That the roof's been looked at. By whom, it didn't say. | Not without being charged for touching their tin. #robbery#.
b : #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_ha# | #bc_true#

scene downs_wind
when topic=memories|land self.away=yes self.from=larkfield
does tell.memory / react.self / assess
a : Up on #downs#, the wind came over the top and took everything with it. Clean. | At #self.from_town# we flew kites off the chalk in #gust#. Mine never came down whole. | I miss a sky with nothing in it. In #here.town#, chimneys fill every gap.
b[from=larkfield] #lark : Took your washing with it, too. | Every kite on the downs came home in pieces. | Nothing but larks and weather.
b : Do you miss it? | Never whole? | Always?
a@lark : Took mine twice in one week. I'd forgotten that. | Mine was in more pieces than most. I mended it with #solder# once. | Larks and weather. And fair price at the cross on Marketday.
a : The wind, yes. Not the walk to the well. | I mended it every time. That's where I started. | Always. Even on the Sump steps you can't see the sky for washing.

scene market_cross_home
when topic=memories|lore self.away=yes self.from=larkfield
does tell.memory / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : There was a tinker at the #self.from_town# market cross every Marketday. I sat and watched him all morning. | My #mum# took every cracked pan we owned to that tinker. He knew our pans better than we did. | The tinker at home said a mend should outlast the pot. I believed him. I still do.
b : What did you learn? | Better than you? | Does it?
a : To look before touching. He'd turn a pot over a full minute before he picked up #iron#. | Every dent. Which one my #dad# dropped and which one I did. | A good one does. The pot gives up round it. I've seen it happen.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_ha# | #bc_true#

scene city_voice
when topic=accent self.away=yes self.from=larkfield
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : They hear #downs# in me the minute I name a price. Fair weight, I say, and they grin. | Up the hill they think a #self.from_town# voice is a joke. It's a voice that won't be short-changed. | I've been here years and I still say tea for supper. I'll not stop.
b : It suits you. | Good for you. | Why stop?
a : #thanks#. It suits #till# too. Nobody tries to diddle a voice like mine twice. | Folk who laugh pay the same as anyone. | I won't. It's mine.

scene gossip_dry
when topic=gossip
does tell.gossip / react.news / insist / ack
a : I hear plenty over #bench#. Folk forget a tinker's ears work. | There's talk about #topic.subject#. Carry it yourself; I'll not. | Half of what's said in #shop# is about somebody who isn't in it.
b : Go on. | Why not? | And the other half?
a : No. I mend #pots#, not reputations. | Because talk's #pot# with #crack# in it. It leaks on whoever's holding it. | About the price of tin. Same as ever. #dry#.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_wise# | #bc_ha#

scene theft_word
when topic=crime|gossip topic.claim_kind=theft
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'm locking the tins up. | Word on #sumpsteps# is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Keep your shutters fast. | #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#, so they're saying. Down #sumpsteps# that'll mean a search.
b : #bc_poor_soul# | I will. | #doubt#.
a : Lock up what you'd miss, #address#. Little else to be done. | Good. And mind the back door; that's the one they use. | Doubt it all you like. The Watch won't.

scene newcomer_pots
when topic=newcomer|player
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : A new face came in this morning, didn't ask the price, didn't ask the time. Just stood. | Strangers bring me the oddest things. A helmet today. I mended it. | I don't mind #stranger#. One with a leaking pot is a customer.
b : What did you do? | A helmet? | Fair enough.
a : Mended a pan and waited. They bought nothing and said thanks. I'll take it. | It was #pot# once, I think. It holds water now, anyway. | Fair weight. The rest can stay strangers.

scene chapel_bread
when topic=faith
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : The chapel gave us bread in a bad week. I've not forgotten it, and I'll not. | I'm no great prayer. The plate gets my odd copper, and the chapel's urn gets mended free. | The Bright One and I have an arrangement. I mend things; the Bright One mends the rest.
b : That's fair. | For nothing? | That's a good arrangement.
a : It's more than fair weight. It's owed. | Every time. It's not a sermon, but it's what I've got. | Most days it works. The other days I mend twice. #dry#.

scene burnt_paw
when topic=health self.job=crafter
does tell.self / react.self / reassure
a : #iron.capitalize# caught my paw again. Third time this month. | My eyes aren't what they were for the fine seams. I squint like an old owl. | Solder fumes give me a head by dusk. I open the door and let #mizzle# in. Worse.
b : Butter on it. | Get some spectacles. | Bad trade for the head.
a : Butter's for bread. Cold water and a curse. #oath#. | With what #coppers#? They're in #jar#. | Bad trade for everything but #till#.

scene cat_on_bench
when topic=animals self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : There's a cat sleeps on the warm end of #bench#. I don't feed it. It doesn't ask. | A rat ran across #shop# this morning with my last rivet. I want it back. | The gulls sit on #shutters# and scream at the fish stall. I side with the gulls.
b : You like it, then. | Your last one? | Why the gulls?
a : I tolerate it. It tolerates me. Fair price both ways. | The last copper one. I've tin. Tin's not the same. | They're honest about wanting the fish. More than I can say for some.

scene dawn_shutters
when topic=day here.time=dawn self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Shutters up before the bells. If I'm late, somebody's porridge goes cold in a cracked pan. | An hour after dawn, the street hushes. Even the alehouse sleeps. | First thing I do is count the #pots# left overnight. Then the coppers. Then the tea.
b : That's early. | Even the alehouse? | Tea last?
a : Early's when they come, on the way to work. I'd lose half my trade lying in. | For about an hour. Then it's #racket# again. | Tea last. You have to earn it. #saying#.

scene friend_kept
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.friend# is the one I'd lend my last rivet to. That's not a small thing, from me. | I'd trust #self.friend# with #till# and my secrets. Both, and I don't say that lightly. | #self.friend# and I have known each other long enough not to need the talking.
b : That's a real friend. | Both? | Lucky.
a : It is. I've not got many. I don't need many, #address#. | Both. And neither's ever come back light. | Lucky, and I know it. Full weight.

scene neighbour_nod
when topic=friends|home self.neighbour
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.neighbour# hears me through the wall, I'm sure of it. We both pretend not. | If #shutters# stuck, #self.neighbour# is who I'd shout for. | I mend #self.neighbour#'s #pots# for nothing. It's cheaper than a quarrel.
b : That's neighbours. | Would they come? | Fair trade.
a : It is. Polite deafness. It's how the Sump keeps the peace. | I'd think so. I'd go the other way. That's the deal. | Fair weight. And they know it.

story first_mend [topic=memories|work self.age>=20]
  : My #gran# had #kettle# with a hole in the spout. I was a #child#, and I mended it with a pin and a lump of wax.
  : It held for one boil. One. Then it hissed all over the stove and put the fire out.
  : She laughed till she cried and gave me a copper for trying. I've still got it.
  ask : Have you still got the first thing you ever made?
  b[old] : I don't. I wish I'd thought to.
  b : No. I'd not have kept mine; it was terrible.

story the_stamped_pan [topic=work|prices self.job=crafter self.age>=25]
  : A pan came to me once with the finest House stamp I'd ever seen. Gilt in the letters.
  : The seam was glued. Glued, not soldered. It came apart in my paws like wet bread.
  : I mended it properly and kept #stamp#. It's nailed over #bench# to remind me.
  ask : Seeing that gilt, would you have bought it?
  b : I would. That's what the gilt is for.
  b[role=merchant] : I'd have sold it. That's worse.

story millpond_dare [topic=pastimes|memories self.away=yes self.from=larkfield self.age>=20]
  : At home in #self.from_town# there was a dare: swim across the mill pond in autumn, before the leaves were down.
  : I went in at dusk with half the street watching. Halfway, my legs stopped answering.
  : I floated on my back and kicked with my arms till I touched the far side. Nobody counted that as cheating.
  b : That's not cheating. Did anyone else make it?
  : Nobody else tried that year. I was the dare for a while.

story the_singer [topic=home|memories here.region=ridgemere self.age>=25]
  : There was a drunk sang under my window every night one winter. The same song, the same wrong note.
  : One night I went down with a pot of tea and told him the right note.
  : He sang it right the next night, and every night after, and I liked it less.
  b : Ha! Should've left the wrong note. Did he ever stop?
  : Come spring, he moved on. I missed him, wrong note and all.

story the_first_copper [topic=dreams|work self.role=merchant]
  : The first copper I put in #jar# was off a bucket I mended for a pup who'd lost the handle.
  : The pup paid me in #coppers# that smelled of fish. I didn't wash them.
  : I've never spent that one. It's at the bottom, under all the others.
  ask : Have you ever kept a coin you'd never spend?
  b : I've never had one spare enough to keep.
  b[wealth=rich] : A few. Not with a story like that.

rule @bench_bark
when concept=bark working=yes self.job=crafter
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Pots mended, kettles patched, fair price. | Holes stopped, handles back on. Fair price.

rule @stamp_bark
when concept=bark working=yes self.job=crafter
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Let's see #stamp#. #dry#. Thought so. | #oath#. That House again, and that seam.

rule @mizzle_bark
when concept=bark here.outdoors=rain self.dislikes=drizzle
does aside
cooldown 8h
say #mizzle.capitalize#. Again. | Shut the door on #mizzle#, somebody.

rule @racket_bark
when concept=bark here.time=night self.dislikes=night_noise
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Some of us open #shutters# at dawn. | #racket.capitalize#. Bright One give me patience.

rule @till_bark
when concept=bark self.role=merchant here.time=dusk
does aside
cooldown 10h
say One for #till#, one for #jar#. | Coppers, #coppers#. #heavytill.capitalize#, maybe.

rule @gust_bark
when concept=bark here.season=autumn here.outdoors=clear|overcast
does aside
cooldown 10h
say There's #gust#. Hold your hats. | #gust.capitalize#. That's better.

dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. Pots, pans, kettles, buckets. If it held water once, I'll make it again. | #greet#. Set it down and I'll tell you if it's worth it.
dlg @2 shop.greet [topic.meeting=known here.shop!=bare] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Not the same kettle, I hope. | #topic.player#. Back again. What's short-weighed you now?
dlg @3 shop.greet [pair.disposition=hostile|wary here.shop!=bare] : Name it. I'll name what it costs. Fair price, no more.
dlg @4 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #greet#. #shop_bare# Nothing mended to sell, but I'll buy old tin.
dlg @1 shop.prices : A patch is a copper, a seam's three, a new bottom's dear. #saying#. | #shop_dear# Tin doesn't come cheap down here.
dlg @1 shop.bye : #farewell#. Read #stamp# before you buy, mind. | #shop_bye# Don't drop it again.
dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. Make it short; #iron#'s hot. | #greet#. What's leaking?
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. You again. I don't mind it.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's late. If #pot#'s not leaking, it'll keep till morning.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : I don't know your face. Stand where the light is and say what you want.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=crafter] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Pots, pans, kettles, anything tin. I read #stamp# and stop the hole. | I mend. Every hole on the street comes to my bench sooner or later.
dlg @2 resident.job : Honest #work#, fair price, and I'm paid on the day. That'll do. | This and that, at a fair price. Whatever comes through the door.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : #heavytill.capitalize#. Hush, or it'll hear me. | More holes than hours. I'm not complaining.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=failing] : Light. Folk are keeping their holes. | Badly. I've three #pots# nobody's fetched.
dlg @3 resident.business [business!=thriving|failing] : Steady. A copper in #jar# most nights. | Fair. #till.capitalize#'s not heavy, but it's honest.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Word from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. That's what reached #bench#. | They're saying #world.news#. I mend #pots#; I don't check it.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : All anyone's talking about over #bench# is #world.event_words#.
dlg @3 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Just holes, as ever. | Nothing. A quiet week at #bench#, which means a loud one somewhere.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Don't tell anyone where you heard it.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : #gossip_none# | I mend #pots#. Talk's a pot I don't touch.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Shut the door; #mizzle# gets in.
graft resident.hub
choice Why do you look at the bottom first? -> stamp
node stamp
say The House mark. Every House saves its tin in its own spot, and the mark says where to look. Stamp first, then the crack.
mood calm
free
choice Which House is worst? -> stamp_worst
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node stamp_worst
say I'll not say it in front of a customer. Whichever one's stamp is biggest this year. That's the one saving on tin to pay for it.
choice Fair enough. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice Is it worth mending, or should I buy new? -> worth
node worth
say Show me. If it's a good pot with a bad seam, mend it. If it's a bad pot with a good stamp, buy a plain one and save the coppers.
mood calm
choice Let me see your wares. -> greet
choice What's it cost? -> prices
choice Goodbye. -> bye
